-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROBLEM STATEMENT
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Curriculum content is licensed CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 (see LICENSE-CONTENT). Applying
a Creative Commons license to source code as well would be a mistake: Creative
Commons itself recommends against using its licenses for software, because
they do not address source-versus-object code, patent grants, or warranty
disclaimers.

The example code therefore needs its own license.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONSTRAINTS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. Learners must be able to fork, modify, publish, and showcase their work
     — including in a portfolio shown to prospective employers.
  2. XR Camp wishes to restrict commercial resale of the curriculum and its
     example projects by third parties.
  3. The license must be software-appropriate.
  4. It must be comprehensible to beginners, who will encounter it early.
  5. It must not create friction for accredited or nonprofit teaching use.

Constraints 1 and 2 are in tension. That tension is the decision.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ALTERNATIVES CONSIDERED
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  A. MIT / Apache-2.0 (permissive)
     + Maximum learner freedom; zero friction; universally understood; the
       norm for educational example code.
     + Apache-2.0 adds an explicit patent grant.
     - Permits commercial resale of the example code. Does not satisfy
       constraint 2.

  B. PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0
     + Purpose-built, software-specific noncommercial license.
     + Plain-language and readable.
     + Aligns with the CC BY-NC-SA posture of the content.
     - Not an OSI-approved open source license. "Noncommercial" is inherently
       ambiguous at the edges. May complicate a learner's portfolio use and
       deter some contributors.

  C. Split licensing — permissive code, noncommercial content
     + Learner code freedom is total; the commercial value XR Camp actually
       needs to protect is the curriculum, videos, and lesson narrative, not
       the boilerplate of an accessible HTML page.
     + Removes contributor friction entirely.
     - Third parties could repackage example projects, though without the
       instructional content that gives them value.

  D. Dual licensing (noncommercial by default, commercial license on request)
     + Preserves both freedom and revenue optionality.
     - Highest administrative burden; requires a CLA from contributors.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RECOMMENDATION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Option C — Apache-2.0 for code, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 for content.

Rationale: the example code is deliberately simple, pedagogical boilerplate.
Its standalone commercial value is low, while the cost of restricting it is
high and falls directly on learners — the people the program exists to serve.
The genuinely valuable and defensible assets are the curriculum, instructor
videos, assessments, and lesson narrative, all of which are already covered by
LICENSE-CONTENT and none of which are public. Apache-2.0 additionally supplies
a patent grant and a clear contribution clause, which matters as outside
contributors arrive.

If leadership concludes that example projects must also be protected from
commercial reuse, adopt Option B (PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0) instead, and
accept the portfolio-use ambiguity — documenting an explicit carve-out in
COMMERCIAL_USE.md permitting learners to display and republish their own
coursework.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
RISKS AND MIGRATION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Relicensing after outside contributions have been accepted requires the
consent of every contributor. Decide before the first external pull request
is merged, or adopt a CLA now to preserve the ability to change course later.

